  "slug": "stochastic-gene-96d7",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published framework apply quantitative models to nutrient-limited E. coli metabolism, cell cycle coupling and microbial aggregation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is mechanistic mathematical modelling of bacterial physiology under nutrient constraint, with E. coli as the central organism in two of the three works. One preprint shows that diffusion of cross-feeding metabolites can destabilise a nutrient-poor microbial community through a Turing mechanism, leading to aggregate formation. A second preprint builds a kinetic model of pyruvate metabolism, the TCA cycle and acetate formation in E. coli, with NADH inhibition of pyruvate dehydrogenase and citrate synthase, and calibrates it using Bayesian methods to test competing acetate flux responses. A published framework compares models of replication-division coordination in E. coli and reports that a new double-process model fits data across growth conditions. What is new across these studies is the use of explicit kinetic or spatial models constrained by experimental data to explain physiological behaviour at both single-cell and community scales.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
